<p>A replica is another copy of data. A partition assigns some data to a particular place. Operating either means knowing which changes each place holds, which work may use it and who can still accept a write when something fails.</p>
<p>Follow the shop from <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">Running a relational service</a>. Writer A records Mina’s order 103; standby B receives its changes. We will separate the moments when 103 is acknowledged, durable on B and visible to B’s queries, then replace A without leaving a second authority. The later event-table example asks a different question: whether dividing one table earns its boundary and retention work.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL 18 supplies the executable contract. A disposable native PostgreSQL 18.6 rehearsal checks selected replication, promotion and partition cases below. The four figures are explanatory models; their steps do not run PostgreSQL. Amazon RDS and CockroachDB v25.4 provide two documented service comparisons, without claiming a cloud or distributed-service rehearsal.</p>

<nav aria-label="In this article" class="article-contents"><ol>{sections.map(([id,title])=><li><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a></li>)}</ol></nav>

<h2 id="turn-service-requirements-into-a-placement-decision">Turn service requirements into a placement decision</h2>
<p>Start with a named failure and a testable promise. For loss of one writer machine, the shop requires no lost acknowledged orders and usable order entry within five minutes. The immediate status read must include Mina’s preceding order. Reports may be up to 30 seconds stale. The parent treats complete location loss separately: four hours to recover and at most five minutes of acknowledged changes lost. These invented requirements make different placement decisions possible; “high availability” alone does not.</p>
<table><caption>What the additional place buys</caption><thead><tr><th>Choice</th><th>Requirement it may address</th><th>Work it leaves</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Larger single writer</td><td>More headroom without changing write ownership</td><td>Still one writer failure boundary; check maintenance and recovery time</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Readable replica</td><td>Offload reads with an acceptable freshness contract</td><td>Writes and their log still originate at the writer; reads may lag</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Eligible standby in another failure domain</td><td>Replace a lost writer within the loss and time allowance</td><td>Correct acknowledgement policy, exclusion, promotion and reconnection</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Distributed write ownership</td><td>Spread separable write demand or place ownership near users</td><td>Routing, cross-owner work, movement and coordinated recovery</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Draw applications, writers, replicas and dependencies inside the actual machine, zone and region boundaries. Two servers sharing a failed volume or power supply do not protect against that loss. A second zone with the same unavailable credentials, connection proxy or discovery service can still leave the application unusable. Include the control path used to exclude writers during a failure. Use the parent’s <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/#assign-every-operating-responsibility">responsibility map</a> to assign every transition to the operator, provider or application team.</p>

<h2 id="follow-a-committed-change-to-a-replica">Follow a committed change to a replica</h2>
<p>PostgreSQL <strong>physical replication</strong> sends write-ahead log records describing storage changes to a standby replaying that history. It follows the cluster’s logged database state, including logged schema changes. It needs compatible architecture and major version, with members normally kept at the same release. Unlogged table contents are not a second durable copy on a standby. A standby is therefore more than a destination that happens to contain the same rows.</p>
<p><strong>Logical replication</strong> copies selected table changes through publication and subscription. It can join different major versions and different physical layouts, under their compatibility rules. PostgreSQL 18 does not copy DDL, sequence state or large objects through ordinary table replication; update and delete also need an appropriate replica identity. A subscriber is an independently running database. Applying some table changes does not make it a physical standby that <code>pg_promote()</code> can turn into a complete replacement. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication-restrictions.html">Logical replication’s restrictions</a> determine the missing preparation.</p>
<p>Figure 1 follows physical replication of order 103. Keep <em>Standby flush</em>, advance to <em>Flush WAL</em> and compare the client’s result with B’s rows. Then select <em>Standby replay</em> at that same step.</p>
<TopologyFigure scene="stream" number={1} />
<p>The standby has a durable commit before it has applied that commit to the state its queries read. The native run made this distinction observable by deliberately pausing replay: A, B’s write position and B’s flush position reached <code>0/3007200</code>, while B’s replay position remained <code>0/3000000</code>. A showed 101 and 103; B still showed only 101. Those are one run’s positions, not reusable boundaries.</p>

<h3 id="decide-what-an-acknowledgement-promises">Decide what an acknowledgement promises</h3>
<p>With asynchronous replication, the writer can acknowledge after its own durable commit while the standby has none of that change. PostgreSQL calls this <code>synchronous_commit = local</code> when deliberately bypassing a configured synchronous wait. Leaving <code>synchronous_standby_names</code> empty also means the default <code>on</code> does not wait for any remote copy. The word “on” alone is insufficient evidence of synchronous replication.</p>
<table><caption>PostgreSQL 18 acknowledgement with a required synchronous standby</caption><thead><tr><th>Commit setting</th><th>Additional standby boundary</th><th>What is still unproved</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td><code>remote_write</code></td><td>Commit written to its operating system</td><td>Survival of standby OS failure; replay and query visibility</td></tr>
 <tr><td><code>on</code></td><td>Commit flushed to durable storage</td><td>Replay and query visibility</td></tr>
 <tr><td><code>remote_apply</code></td><td>Commit replayed as well as durably stored</td><td>Freshness of a different replica or an older reader snapshot</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Every mode above also waits for the writer’s own flush. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-wal.html#GUC-SYNCHRONOUS-COMMIT">The WAL settings contract</a> defines these boundaries. The figure’s local choice retains local durability; it does not illustrate <code>off</code>, which can acknowledge before even that flush.</p>
<p>Specify participants too. <code>FIRST 1 (replica)</code> names this drill’s required member. PostgreSQL also supports priority lists and <code>ANY n</code> acknowledgement from a configured set. A reply from any one member does not prove every other promotion candidate has the change. Eligibility must preserve the required acknowledged history; standby count alone is insufficient. These <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-replication.html#GUC-SYNCHRONOUS-STANDBY-NAMES">membership policies</a> belong in the failover contract.</p>
<p>A missing required member makes committing writes wait. In the drill, a disconnected B left the write waiting at <code>SyncRep</code>; reconnecting B allowed acknowledgement. Silently changing to local acknowledgement would restore availability by weakening the shop’s loss promise. A statement timeout, cancellation or broken connection does not establish rollback of a locally committed transaction waiting for replication. Preserve the operation’s identity and hand its outcome to <a href="/review/relational-next/request-recovery/">request recovery</a>.</p>

<h3 id="keep-the-stream-able-to-catch-up">Keep the stream able to catch up</h3>
<p>A backlog before receipt points towards generation, sender or transport limits. A backlog between flushed WAL and replay points towards apply work, recovery conflicts or a deliberately paused standby. A single “seconds behind” number hides that distinction. Bytes measure queued history; elapsed time measures a delay under its metric’s definition; an application read establishes whether a required fact is visible. On an idle database, time since the last replayed transaction can grow even when no change is missing.</p>
<Code code={progress} lang="sql" themes={themes} />
<p>A physical <strong>replication slot</strong> retains history its member may still need. It protects catch-up at the writer’s storage expense. Monitor the slot’s required position, status and available disk time alongside the replica. A finite <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code> bounds retention at checkpoints but can leave the member without required WAL. An unlimited slot can exhaust the writer. Neither choice removes the need for intervention before the storage or recovery budget runs out.</p>
<p>The drill retained 168 bytes between its current position and slot restart position during disconnection; that is position distance, not the size of WAL files on disk. It did not fill storage or invalidate a slot. If required WAL has gone, use an accessible complete archive where supported or reinitialise from a new base. Estimate whether catch-up can outrun incoming work within the allowance. <a href="/review/relational-next/monitoring-and-capacity/">Monitoring and capacity</a> owns the workload and disk budget; never remove WAL files by hand to relieve pressure.</p>

<h2 id="send-each-read-to-a-copy-that-can-answer-it">Send each read to a copy that can answer it</h2>
<p>Mina’s first status read cannot use “usually under 30 seconds behind”. It needs her order now. Route that workflow to the writer as the straightforward policy. Reports can use B while its observed delay remains within their 30-second contract, with a defined response when it exceeds that bound. A transaction needing a consistent view must also use the selected engine’s isolation and snapshot contract; sending its statements to arbitrary copies does not create one view.</p>
<p>A supported progress barrier is another option. On one physical replication history, capture a writer WAL position known to follow the confirmed commit, wait until the chosen standby has replayed at least that position, then open the required read snapshot there. Bound the wait and define writer fallback or an explicit unavailable response. A position captured before commit, a different standby, a pre-existing old snapshot or a different post-failover history defeats that argument. Use a provider’s documented mechanism when it owns replication; do not assume a raw LSN has the same meaning across services.</p>
<p><code>remote_apply</code> moves the replay wait into commit for the participating synchronous members. It does not bless an arbitrary load-balanced replica. Bind the routing decision to the member whose progress was established. Refresh that decision when promotion or connection discovery changes the topology.</p>
<p>Readable standby work also competes with replay. PostgreSQL may cancel a standby query when its snapshot conflicts with recovery and the permitted delay is exhausted. <code>hot_standby_feedback</code> can prevent some cleanup conflicts by holding back removal on the writer, at the cost of retained versions there; it does not eliminate every conflict. Set a report cancellation and retry policy consistent with freshness and writer storage. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/hot-standby.html#HOT-STANDBY-CONFLICT">Hot standby conflicts</a> explain this operating trade; query optimisation belongs to <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">performance</a>.</p>

<h2 id="replace-a-writer-without-leaving-two-authorities">Replace a writer without leaving two authorities</h2>
<p>Promotion ends a standby’s recovery and lets it form a new writable history. It does not disable the former writer. <strong>Fencing</strong> is the enforced exclusion that makes that old authority unable to accept writes, including from jobs, administrators and lingering client sessions. A failed health check from one network is evidence of reachability from there, not proof of exclusion.</p>
<p>Figure 2 first follows enforced exclusion. Advance to the route change, then choose <em>Still accepts writes</em>. That branch deliberately violates the promotion gate so the consequence stays visible.</p>
<TopologyFigure scene="promotion" number={2} />
<p>A planned <strong>switchover</strong> can stop admitting writes, resolve in-flight work and establish a final committed boundary before moving authority. Unplanned writer loss may leave that final boundary unavailable. Preserve the available application acknowledgements, member positions and failure evidence; identify the candidate’s recoverable history and the explicit loss decision. If the shop cannot prove its zero-loss boundary, promotion is not automatically acceptable just because B is reachable.</p>
<table><caption>Promotion gates, in order</caption><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Evidence to proceed</th><th>Pause or stop when</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Establish state and budget</td><td>Writer failure distinguished from client-path failure; acknowledgement evidence preserved; time and loss allowance named</td><td>Cause, authority or permitted loss is unresolved</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Select a candidate</td><td>Compatible member, recoverable position and required history; replay state, dependencies and capacity checked</td><td>Missing history, paused replay or loss outside the allowance</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Exclude former authority</td><td>Supported fence enforced and restart or automatic failback prevented across all writer paths</td><td>Old writer might still accept writes</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Promote</td><td>Supported engine or provider operation completes; candidate is writable on its new history</td><td>Operation uncertain, failed or candidate not ready</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Reconnect and validate</td><td>New connections reach the intended writer; controlled write/read and required facts pass</td><td>Wrong destination, missing data, failed workflow or excess load</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>The disposable switchover used an explicitly stopped A as its exclusion. A production system needs a fence that remains enforced under restart, network partition and automated controllers, such as the documented infrastructure or cluster-manager mechanism. PostgreSQL itself does not supply the external failure detector and exclusion policy. Its <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/warm-standby-failover.html">failover documentation</a> makes that operating responsibility explicit.</p>
<Code code={promotion} lang="sh" themes={themes} />

<h3 id="reconnect-clients-and-establish-the-result">Reconnect clients and establish the result</h3>
<p>A stable writer hostname is a discovery promise, not a surviving database session. DNS caches, proxies and pools can preserve old destinations or existing connections after a route changes. Refresh discovery according to the deployed client contract, discard affected sessions and reconnect within a bounded admission policy. Check the server reached by new connections and its intended writer role before reopening ordinary traffic.</p>
<p>Then write a controlled identifiable order under the application identity, commit it and perform its required status read through the real routing path. Compare known acknowledged work with the promoted data. Record the interruption through usable service, lost acknowledged changes and unresolved requests separately. The harness checked fresh <code>psql</code> connections and a new order; it did not exercise the shop’s real pool, proxy or permissions. <a href="/review/relational-next/connections/">Connection lifetimes</a> and <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/#recover-according-to-what-the-application-knows">application recovery</a> own client disposal and uncertain-operation reconciliation.</p>

<h3 id="rejoin-or-replace-the-former-member">Rejoin or replace the former member</h3>
<p>Keep A excluded when it returns. If its history never diverged and the supported procedure can follow the new timeline with all required WAL, catch-up may be possible. If it accepted writes on its own branch, pointing it at B does not merge the branches. Preserve the old history for loss investigation before any procedure discards it.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/app-pgrewind.html"><code>pg_rewind</code></a> can bring a diverged former member onto the source history when its prerequisites hold, including checksums or <code>wal_log_hints</code>, <code>full_page_writes</code> and required history. It discards target-branch changes; it does not reconcile them. Check its target shutdown, WAL access and configuration requirements. Otherwise replace the member from an authorised fresh source, using <a href="/review/relational-next/backup-and-restore/">the restore procedure</a> where a backup supplies it.</p>
<p>The drill chose replacement: A stayed stopped, its disposable data directory was replaced from B with standby configuration, and A rejoined read-only. It replayed new order 109 from B, and an insert attempted on A was rejected. Before readmission in service, verify source and timeline, read-only role, streaming and replay progress, retention, application routing and recovery capacity. Removing the fence before those checks recreates the original hazard.</p>

<h2 id="choose-between-local-partitions-and-distributed-ownership">Choose between local partitions and distributed ownership</h2>
<p>The shop’s append-oriented operational events have a date, an identifier and a retention decision. A local partition divides that table into date ranges under the same PostgreSQL service. An October row belongs in October’s table; a November filter can avoid October entirely. Reads, transactions and writer failure still belong to the same database authority.</p>
<p>Choose the date’s meaning first: here <code>day</code> is the event’s agreed UTC calendar date, not arrival time. Monthly half-open boundaries include the first day and exclude the first day of the next month. Late arrival can need an old partition; a corrected event date can move a row between partitions. A busy current month can still be hot even if old months are evenly sized.</p>
<Code code={partition} lang="sql" themes={themes} />
<p>This native fixture’s November query planned a scan of <code>events_nov</code> only. That proves pruning for this predicate and these bounds; it supplies no throughput or retention-duration result. Check useful work saved with representative data and plans, including queries without a date and prepared-query behaviour, using <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">performance diagnosis</a>.</p>
<p>The key also changed. PostgreSQL 18 requires parent uniqueness to include the partition key; the fixture rejected <code>UNIQUE (id)</code> and accepted ID 7 on two dates. If the real event identifier must be globally unique, <code>PRIMARY KEY (day,id)</code> does not preserve that rule. References to that key must carry the date too, or an explicitly designed separate identity relation must own the global identity. A mutable date in a referenced key brings further update duties. <a href="/review/relational-next/constraints/">Constraints</a> owns the meaning and enforcement design; partitioning cannot silently weaken it.</p>
<p>Figure 3 tries a missing date, then prepares its boundary. Compare a late arrival with the date correction. At retirement, inspect what remains in service and what is merely detached.</p>
<TopologyFigure scene="partitions" number={3} />
<p>PostgreSQL rejects a row with no matching partition unless a suitable default partition exists. Decide deliberately whether late data is rejected, held for approval or accepted into retained coverage. A default can keep ingestion running but adds a monitored holding area that must be drained and checked before overlapping ranges can be attached. Future boundaries need an owner, advance creation and an alert before coverage runs out. Corrections need destination coverage and all affected key and relationship checks; our date update physically moved its row from <code>events_oct</code> to <code>events_nov</code>.</p>
<p>For one monthly retirement, proceed only when every row qualifies under the retention rule, exceptions and dependent references are resolved, required archives are verified, and continuing writers cannot send permitted work into the removed range. Check the partition’s actual bounds, schema, indexes and dependencies. Pause if late-write policy, legal retention, references or required evidence is unresolved. After detachment, verify parent queries and new writes, the detached dataset and its access and expiry owner. Detachment retains the table; it is neither file erasure nor proof that backups have expired.</p>
<p>For attachment, require non-overlapping bounds, compatible columns and constraints, verified in-range data, suitable indexes and checked default-partition contents. Declare completion only after catalogue coverage and representative boundary writes and reads agree. Use the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-partitioning.html">selected engine’s partition contract</a>; <a href="/review/relational-next/schema-changes/">schema changes</a> owns locks and physical execution, <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/#retire-old-data-and-prove-the-change-is-finished">live retirement</a> owns the data lifecycle, and monitoring owns capacity and missed-boundary alerts.</p>
<p><strong>Decision for this observed case: retain an ordinary unpartitioned table.</strong> A tiny fixture and one pruned plan do not demonstrate a benefit worth the new coverage, identity and retirement duties. Reconsider when measured date-bounded work or whole-period retirement exceeds the ordinary table’s budget, with the invariants preserved and an assigned lifecycle owner. A large row count alone does not settle it.</p>

<h3 id="choose-a-key-and-trace-work-across-owners">Choose a key and trace work across owners</h3>
<p>Distributed ownership addresses a different limit. Suppose independent tenants’ order creation has exhausted the tested writer capacity after query and workload improvements, while reads are already offloaded. Another read replica still leaves every order and stock update at that writer. Tenant ownership might separate that demand if orders, items and stock are tenant-local. A single globally contended inventory row would remain a hot dependency.</p>
<p>Figure 4 traces a tenant order. Then select the report without a tenant key and the transfer between tenants. Count how many authorities the work needs.</p>
<TopologyFigure scene="owners" number={4} />
<p>With application-managed PostgreSQL shards, the application or a routing layer chooses an independent database. Include the tenant key in the intended uniqueness and reference rules; an <code>id</code> unique on each shard is not automatically globally unique. Colocate related facts so ordinary joins and transactions remain within one owner. Define placement for shared tables and operations missing the key. Distribution by tenant does not split one oversized tenant, and a changing tenant key becomes a data move.</p>
<p>Separate connections to two shards do not provide one atomic transaction or one cross-shard snapshot. A transfer needs a deliberately supported cross-database protocol or a revised workflow with its own recovery semantics. An integrated distributed relational service can supply cross-placement SQL transactions under its documented contract, while adding network coordination and failure dependencies. Keep transaction correctness with <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">transactions</a>; use its guarantees here to decide whether the placement fits the actual workload.</p>

<h3 id="move-ownership-and-retain-a-recovery-path">Move ownership and retain a recovery path</h3>
<p>Growth, skew and locality can require moving tenant 7. Record who owns writes before, during and after cutover, how the destination obtains a consistent state, the final boundary it must apply, and how routing and old sessions are changed. Until the destination accepts writes, abort may leave the source authoritative. Afterwards, returning requires carrying the new history back or recovering forward. A route reversal alone loses access to accepted work.</p>
<p>Budget spare destination storage, copy and catch-up bandwidth, degraded workload capacity and per-owner replica protection. A move that saturates the only surviving replica can weaken recovery precisely during the transition. Require data and routing validation, a stop condition and a recovery owner. <a href="/review/relational-next/database-moves/">Controlled database moves</a> supplies the live procedure.</p>
<p>Recovery is now a placement decision too. Independently restoring two owners to different boundaries can reconstruct a transfer on one side only. Specify a supported consistent recovery boundary or an application reconciliation method before accepting cross-owner invariants. <a href="/review/relational-next/backup-and-restore/">Restoration</a> establishes recoverable inputs and facts; it cannot manufacture an undocumented global snapshot.</p>

<h2 id="check-the-actual-hosting-and-distribution-contract">Check the actual hosting and distribution contract</h2>
<p>Amazon RDS <strong>Multi-AZ DB instance</strong> deployment provisions a synchronous standby in a different Availability Zone and manages failover. That standby does not serve read traffic. A read replica and a Multi-AZ DB cluster are different deployment products. Record the exact engine, deployment type, regions and settings before assigning any of their guarantees to the shop.</p>
<p>For this instance arrangement, inspect failure triggers and events, the supported reboot-with-failover action, recovery behaviour and client DNS handling. RDS documents that failover updates the instance DNS record and requires existing connections to be re-established. The provider manages the infrastructure transition; the customer still validates application results and reconnects clients. This zone arrangement alone establishes no cross-region or account-loss recovery promise. The <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Concepts.MultiAZSingleStandby.html">deployment description</a> and <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_RebootInstance.html">reboot contract</a> are the specific references, rather than the general “Multi-AZ” label.</p>
<p>CockroachDB v25.4 instead divides keyspace into replicated ranges and supplies integrated SQL transactions across them. Its <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v25.4/architecture/transaction-layer">transaction layer</a> defaults to Serializable isolation and also supports Read Committed; verify the application’s actual setting. Its <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v25.4/multiregion-overview">multi-region contract</a> distinguishes survival goals from table localities. A locality choice concerns where work is favoured; a survival goal concerns the failures to tolerate. Neither “three nodes” nor “multi-region” alone establishes the configured combination.</p>
<p>For an integrated service, identify which replicas participate in writes, who can serve a current or intentionally stale read, how ranges and authority move, what happens when the configured failure tolerance is exceeded, and who repairs lost capacity. Measure the latency of local and cross-region work under that layout. More distant acknowledgement participants can protect a larger failure boundary while adding travel to commit. <a href="/review/relational-next/cockroachdb/">The product assessment</a> develops its adoption checks; the local PostgreSQL drill provides no evidence of that service’s performance or regional survival.</p>

<h2 id="rehearse-the-failures-that-determine-the-choice">Rehearse the failures that determine the choice</h2>
<p><a href={rehearsalUrl} download="rehearse.py">Download the disposable topology rehearsal</a>. It requires Python 3 and PostgreSQL 18 server tools, runs as an ordinary OS user and refuses to overwrite an evidence file. It creates only fresh temporary clusters on unused loopback ports. It contacts no existing service, retains commands, outputs and server logs, then stops its own servers and removes its temporary data.</p>
<Code code={run} lang="sh" themes={themes} />
<p>Before each fault the harness establishes a tiny known order set. Commands and waits have finite limits. A failed assertion, a 12-second condition deadline or a tool timeout aborts the drill into cleanup. Its connection fault changes only its standby’s source address to an unused local port; its writer-loss fault uses immediate shutdown of a separate disposable writer. It does not alter host networking or other databases.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Native PostgreSQL 18.6 rehearsal outcomes"><table><thead><tr><th>Fault or transition</th><th>Observed evidence</th><th>Decision supported</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Pause standby replay</td><td>103 acknowledged with standby flush; absent from standby query. <code>remote_apply</code> waited at <code>SyncRep</code> until replay resumed.</td><td>Flush acknowledgement alone cannot route Mina’s immediate read there</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Break replica connectivity</td><td>Local-acknowledged 105 missing on B; required synchronous 106 waited. Slot retained history; reconnection caught up and released the wait.</td><td>Missing synchronous member is an availability decision, not an automatic loss-policy change</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Planned switchover</td><td>Final replay boundary checked; A stopped before promotion. B retained 101, 103–106 and accepted 107. Old endpoint rejected connection.</td><td>Controlled local exclusion, promotion and fresh-connection path worked</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Former member returns</td><td>Old directory replaced from B before startup; <code>pg_is_in_recovery()</code> true; insert rejected as read-only</td><td>This replacement rejoined as a standby; rewind and history reconciliation untested</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Disconnected standby, then writer loss</td><td>A acknowledged 201 while B was stopped. A was stopped immediately; promoted B lacked 201.</td><td>Asynchronous arrangement fails the shop’s zero-loss machine-failure requirement</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Partition boundaries and correction</td><td>Missing September/December rejected; <code>UNIQUE(id)</code> rejected; date correction moved tables; November scan pruned October; December attachment admitted its boundary row; detach retained one October row separately</td><td>Routing and enforcement cases established; benefit at service scale unproved</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The planned local interval from stopping A to B’s accepted new write was 0.303 seconds. The crash comparison took 0.252 seconds from starting its standby to promotion. Both are tiny loopback observations with warm local storage, manual authority control and no failure detection, remote fault domain, proxy, application pool or real traffic. Neither measures the shop’s five-minute end-to-end interruption.</p>
<p>Accept the real deployment only after its representative rehearsal records every issued and acknowledged operation, surviving facts, candidate history, enforced authority, fresh connections, required reads, retention pressure and the complete interruption. Include planned switchover, writer loss, broken replica connectivity and a returning former writer. For distributed ownership, also rehearse the relevant owner or location loss and coordinated recovery under the chosen service. Test the actual provider transition where it owns the mechanism.</p>
<p>The shop’s current conclusion is conditional: keep one write owner; use a verified synchronous eligible standby for its machine-loss promise, writer routing for immediate order status and separately bounded replica reports. Reject asynchronous promotion as proof of zero loss. Keep the ordinary event table until measured benefit justifies partition lifecycle work. Revisit distributed ownership only with evidence that separable write demand or locality requires it and that cross-owner correctness and recovery remain affordable.</p>

